<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-intro-share" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Invitation to Share our Discoveries</title>
    <!--Might move into chapter on area near area formulas assignment-->
  <subsection>
    <title>Identifying a Pattern</title>
    <p>
      In this exploration, you will begin by performing computations. The results should form 
      a pattern that you can use to formulate a conjecture. 
    </p>
    <exploration xml:id="sum-odds">
      <task>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Compute the following sums:
        </p>
      </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
                <m>1+3</m>
              </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>1+3+5</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>1+3+5+7</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>1+3+5+7+9</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </task>
      <task>
        <introduction>
        <p>
          Without actually adding the values, make a prediction for the 
          following sums:
        </p>
        </introduction>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                <m>1+3+5+\cdots +11</m>
              </p>
            </statement>

          </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                <m>1+3+5+\cdots + 41</m>
              </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>
                The expression <m>1+3+5+\cdots + 11</m> is another way of writing 
                <m>1+3+5+7+9+11</m>. We write enough addends to establish a pattern  
                and then the <m>\cdots </m> tells the reader that the pattern continues.
                The <m>+11</m> at the end indicates that the sum will end when the pattern 
                reaches <m>11</m>. 
              </p>
              <p>
                Observe the use of <m>2n-1</m> to represent the final addend in the sum. For example, 
                when the final addend is <m>11</m>, we see that the sum is equal to <m>n^2</m>. How does 
                the sum relate to <m>n</m> in each case?
              </p>
            </hint>
          </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                <m>1+3+5+\cdots +201</m>
              </p>
            </statement>
          </task>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Based on your work above, give a simple formula that can be used to 
              calculate <m>1+3+5+\cdots+(2n-1)</m> for any positive integer <m>n</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint>
            <p>
              In the previous problem, <m>n=100</m> since <m>201=2(100)+1</m>. The variable <m>n</m> will also appear in the formula for the sum.
            </p>
          </hint>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Discuss how you came up with your formula, and explain why you believe it 
              holds for all <m>n\geq 2</m>. If you were not able to come up with 
              a formula, describe what you have discovered about these sums.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
    </exploration>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-explainconjectures">
    <title>Conjectures and Explanations in Mathematics</title>
    <p>
      Have you ever wondered how mathematics came into existence? A simple answer is 
      that people explored mathematical objects, like numbers or shapes, and noticed 
      patterns and consistencies. They then sought to convince themselves and others that 
      these patterns were universal properties of the 
      numeric or geometric structure, not just coincidences. For example, they observed and then proved that 
      whenever a triangle had two sides of equal length the angles opposite those sides 
      must also have the same measures.
    </p>
    <p>
      How do we convince others? In mathematics, we start with basic assumptions like 
      the principles and definitions in this text  
      and then use logic to demonstrate that our conjecture must follow. We shall also accept 
      the algebraic properties of the 
      real number system and properties of equality and inequality, allowing us  
      to simplify algebraic expressions and solve linear equalities and inequalities  
      using techniques learned in previous courses. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Once we prove a claim, it is no longer a conjecture and is now considered to be a theorem. We can then add the theorem to 
      our toolbox and use it to demonstrate new claims.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since this textbook is written for students majoring in fields other than mathematics, we will not 
      spend time developing proof-writing techniques. Instead, we will focus on writing convincing arguments. Everyone can benefit from learning to 
      write clear and thorough explanations and demonstrations. Future teachers should develop the 
      ability to explain mathematics carefully and accurately, using words, pictures, and multiple approaches 
      that their students can understand. For a future lawyer or business professional, the practice of writing 
      explanatory papers in mathematics will enhance their ability to 
      present and support arguments for their cases and proposals. As you write explanations, choose an 
      audience that makes sense for you--a student, a friend, a colleague, a jury
      --and use language appropriate for that audience.
    </p>
    <p>
      How do we write mathematical explanations and establish new truths? 
      We usually let our readers know  
      what we claim and what we plan to prove. In geometry, we often accompany our explanations with a sketch on which 
      we have labeled with the names of points and/or variables for lengths so that our reader can follow our 
      argument visually. We assign variables instead of numerical values to lengths so we do not limit our claim to 
      one or two examples. 
    </p>
    <p>
      In <xref ref="sum-odds"/>, we discovered a formula for the sum 
      of the first <m>n</m> odd numbers. The sum appears to equal <m>n^2</m>, but why? 
      Simply observing the pattern via a few examples, does not convince us that 
      this will also be the case for <m>n=1000</m>. Something strange might happen 
      between 5 and 1000. Instead, consider the following <q>proof by pictures</q>:
    </p>
    <exploration xml:id="pattern-dissected-squares">
      <introduction>
        <p>
          The first four steps of a visual pattern are provided. 
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="sumofoddnumsfig">
          <caption>
            A geometric pattern.
          </caption>
          <image source="sumofoddnums.svg" xml:id="sumofoddnums-web">
            <description>
              First, a 1 by 1 square. Second, four 1 by 1 squares 
              arranged to form a 2 by 2 square. Third, a 2 by 2 square with a 
              horizontal 1 by 2 rectangle added to the bottom and a vertical 
              2 by 1 rectangle added to the right and a 1 unit square in the 
              lower right corner filled in. Finally, a 3 by 3 square has a 
              1 by 3 rectangle added to the bottom edge, a 3 by 1 rectangle  
              added along the right edge, and a 1 unit square in the lower  
              right corner.
            </description>
          </image>
          <!--<image component="print" source="sumofoddnums.png">
            <description>
              First, a 1 by 1 square. Second, four 1 by 1 squares 
              arranged to form a 2 by 2 square. Third, a 2 by 2 square with a 
              horizontal 1 by 2 rectangle added to the bottom and a vertical 
              2 by 1 rectangle added to the right and a 1 unit square in the 
              lower right corner filled in. Finally, a 3 by 3 square has a 
              1 by 3 rectangle added to the bottom edge, a 3 by 1 rectangle  
              added along the right edge, and a 1 unit square in the lower  
              right corner.
            </description>
          </image>-->
        </figure>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Sketch what the fifth and sixth step of the pattern should look 
            like.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Since each of the figures in <xref ref="sumofoddnumsfig"/> is a square, we can measure
            the height and width and multiply the two numbers to get the area. For step one, we get <m>1\times 1=1</m>, 
            and for step two, we get <m>2\times 2=4</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Use this technique to determine the area of each of the following figures:
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p> The third figure.</p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>The fourth figure.</p>
          </statement> 
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement> 
            <p> The fifth figure.</p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The sixth figure.
            </p>
          </statement> 
        </task>
      </task>
      <task>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Since our goal is not to compute the areas, but rather to show an equality of two expressions, 
            let's also express the area of each figure in terms of the sum of its parts. The area of the third 
            figure in <xref ref="sumofoddnumsfig"/> can be found by determining the 
            area of each of the four rectangles (blue, tan, tan, and red) that make up the figure and adding
            these four areas, namely <m>2^2+2+2+1</m>. 
          </p>
          <p>
              Compute the areas of each figure showing the four smaller areas that contribute to the sum.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The fourth figure.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The fifth figure.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The sixth figure.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </task>
      <task>
        <introduction>
          <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
            <p>
              The image to the right shows an <m>n\times n</m>-square with 
              two <m>n\times 1</m> rectangles aligned with the lower and 
              right sides, respectively. The lower right corner has been 
              filled with a 1-unit square.
            </p>
            <figure>
              <caption>
                An arbitrary step in the pattern.
              </caption>
              <image component="web" source="nthsumofodds.svg"></image>
              <!--<image component="print" source="nthsumofodds.png"></image>-->
            </figure>
          </sidebyside>
          <p>
            Write the following as unsimplified algebraic expressions with the variable <m>n</m>:
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The total area found by multiplying the height and length of the figure.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>         
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The total area found by adding the areas of the four rectangles.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            What algebraic equation or formula does this work suggest? Give an explanation 
            of why it must hold using words, pictures, geometry, and/or algebra.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exploration>
  </subsection>    
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-letter">
    <title>A Sample Explanatory Letter</title>
    <p>
      The following letter demonstrates how we might write a thorough, yet informal, 
      explanatory letter for <xref ref="pattern-dissected-squares"/>. Note the use of 
      variables for a general argument and the use of labeled 
      sketches to help the reader follow our argument.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Dear Aunt Josephine,
      </p>
      <p>
        I have been thinking about you a lot lately and how much I appreciated 
        your help when I was learning algebra. You may remember how much I struggled 
        with understanding why <m>(x+1)^2</m> equals <m>x^2+2x+1</m> instead of 
        <m>x^2+1</m> in that course. In my current geometry course, I learned a 
        geometric argument for that equation that makes more sense to me and I thought 
        you might like it too.
      </p>
      <p>
          In the picture below, you can see a square with sides of length <m>x+1</m> 
          broken up into four rectangles. 
          The area of this square is <m>(x+1)^2</m> of course. Notice that <m>x^2+1</m>
          is the sum of the areas of the large <m>x\times x</m> square (top right) and 
          the small <m>1\times 1</m> square (lower left). These do not fill the 
          entire square so <m>(x+1)^2</m> must be greater than <m>x^2+1</m>. The two missing pieces are 
          rectangles with area <m>x\times 1=x</m>. Adding all four pieces, we see  
          <m>(x+1)^2=x^2+2x+1</m>.
      </p>
      </blockquote>

      <figure xml:id="prefig-Josephine-square">
        <caption>Visualization of <m>(x+1)^2=x^2+2x+1</m>, authored in <prefigure/>.</caption>
        <image width="40%">
          <shortdescription></shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-Josephine-sq">
            <diagram dimensions="(200, 200)" margins="5">
              <coordinates bbox="(-5, -5, 5, 5)">
                <rectangle lower-left="(-4,-3)" dimensions="(1,7)" fill="tan" stroke="black" stroke-width="0.5"/>
                <rectangle lower-left="(-3,-3)" dimensions="(7,7)" fill="blue" stroke="black" stroke-width="0.5"/>
                <rectangle lower-left="(-4,-4)" dimensions="(1,1)" fill="red" stroke="black" stroke-width="0.5"/>
                <rectangle lower-left="(-3,-4)" dimensions="(7,1)" fill="tan" stroke="black" stroke-width="0.5"/>
               <label p="(-4,0.5)" alignment="w" size="1.5"> <m>x</m> </label>
                <label p="(-4,-3.5)" alignment="w" size="1"> <m>1</m> </label>
                <label p="(-3.5,-4)" alignment="s" size="1"> <m>1</m> </label>
                <label p="(0.5,-4)" alignment="s" size="1.5"> <m>x</m> </label>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <blockquote>
          <p>
        Now that I have seen this geometrically I remember the formula much better!
      </p>
      <p>
        I hope you and Uncle Tom are doing well. I look forward to visiting you 
        soon.
      </p>
      <p>
        Syd
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Notice how the author Syd introduces the reader to what will be demonstrated, 
      uses a picture to help the reader visualize the explanation, and then explains  
      in words how the picture justifies the argument. Since the author is  
      using the sketch from the activity, a citation reference should be included. In fact, 
      if a tool like Polypad, GeoGebra, or PreFigure is used to create a sketch, the technology 
      should be credited. Also, note how Aunt Josephine did not have to see the questions 
      in the activity to understand Syd's explanation. 
    </p>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Did you notice the unstated use of <xref ref="principle-areasums"/> in Syd's 
        letter? Explain the role of this principle here.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</subsection>
<exercises xml:id="exercises-intro-share">
  <title>Exercises</title>
    <subexercises xml:id="SAR-intro-share">
    <title>Skills and Recall</title>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Determine a likely next term in the sequence <m>5,10,15,20,\ldots</m>. Give a reason for your answer.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The next term is likely <m>25</m> since the difference between consecutive terms is <m>5</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Determine a likely next term in the sequence <m>3,6,12,24,\ldots</m>. Give a reason for your answer.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The next term is likely <m>48</m> since each term is double the previous term.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="SAR-intro-share-twopossibilities">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Determine two different possibilities for the next term in the 
            sequence <m>1,2,4,\ldots</m>. Give reasons for your answers.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            One possibility is <m>8</m>, where each term is double the previous term. 
            Another possibility is <m>7</m>, where the differences between consecutive terms are 1, 2, 3, ...
            Perhaps, there are other possibilities as well.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="SAR-intro-share-500oddsum">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Determine the sum of the first 500 odd numbers. Explain your reasoning.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
          <p>
            See <xref ref="sum-odds"/> for a discussion of the sum of the first <m>n</m> odd numbers.
          </p>
        </hint>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The sum of the first 500 odd numbers is <m>250000</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The sum of the first <m>n</m> odd numbers is <m>n^2</m>. Therefore, the sum of the first 
            500 odd numbers is <m>500^2=250000</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Suppose <m>1+3+5+\cdots +n=144</m>. Determine the value of <m>n</m>. Explain your reasoning.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint>
            <p>
              See <xref ref="sum-odds"/>.
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              The value of <m>n</m> is <m>12</m>.
            </p>
          </answer>
          <solution>
            <p>
              The sum of the first <m>n</m> odd numbers is <m>n^2</m>. Therefore, we have <m>n^2=144</m>, 
              which implies that <m>n=12</m>.
            </p>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Use a sketch and geometric argument to explain why <m>(x+4)(x+3)=x^2+7x+12</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution>
            <p>
              Consider a rectangle with length <m>x+4</m> and width <m>x+3</m>. The area of the rectangle is 
              <m>(x+4)(x+3)</m>. We can divide the rectangle into four smaller rectangles: one with area 
              <m>x^2</m>, two with areas <m>4x</m> and <m>3x</m>, and one with area <m>12</m>. Adding these areas together, we get 
              <m>x^2+4x+3x+12=x^2+7x+12</m>.
            </p>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        
    </subexercises>
    <subexercises xml:id="ExtConc-intro-share">
      <title>Extending the Concepts</title>
      <exercise xml:id="exer-tri-numbers">
        <title>A Triangular Pattern of Dots</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Consider the pattern suggested by the triangles of dots:
          </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-triangular-numbers1">
          <caption>A Sequence of Triangles</caption>
          <image source="trinumbersequilateral">
            <description>
              T1 is a dot. The remaining figures look like equilateral
              triangles. T2 contains a row of two dots and a dot above. T3 adds a 
              row of three dots below T2. T4 adds a row of four dots below T3. 
              T5 adds a row of five dots below T4.
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
              <p>
                List the number of dots in each of <m>T_1, T_2, T_3, T_4, T_5</m>.
                Do not add these five numbers.
              </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>T_1</m> has 1 dot, <m>T_2</m> has 3 dots, <m>T_3</m> has 6 dots, <m>T_4</m> has 10 dots, <m>T_5</m> has 15 dots.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Sketch <m>T_6</m>. How many dots does it have?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>T_6</m> has 21 dots. It can be sketched by adding a row of six dots below <m>T_5</m>.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Without drawing the picture, how many dots should <m>T_{10}</m>
              have? Why?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>T_{10}</m> should have 55 dots. Reasons may vary.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <introduction>
            <p>
              In <xref ref="right-isos-tri-5"/>, the dots in <m>T_5</m> have been  
              rearranged to form an isosceles right triangle, labeled <m>S_5</m>. Then a second copy 
              of this configuration has been made and rotated so that together they 
              form a rectangle, labeled <m>R_5</m>.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="right-isos-tri-5">
              <caption>Rearranging the fifth triangle of dots</caption>
              <image source="trinumbersrightisosceles" width="80%">
                <description>Described in problem.</description>
              </image>
            </figure>
          </introduction>
            <task>
              <statement>
                <p>
                  Use this process to sketch <m>R_2</m>, <m>R_3</m>, and <m>R_4</m>. What should 
                  <m>R_1</m> look like?
                </p>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                  <p>
                    Each rectangle will have height <m>n+1</m> and width <m>n</m>. The two triangles
                    should be visible.
                    Rectangle <m>R_1</m> consists of one column with two dots.
                  </p>
                </answer>
              </task>
              <task>
                <statement>
                  <p>
                    What is the number of dots (blue and red together) in each row of <m>R_5</m>?
                  </p>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                  <p>
                    5
                  </p>
                </answer>
              </task>
              <task>
                <statement>
                  <p>
                    How many rows of dots does <m>R_5</m> have?
                  </p>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                  <p>
                    6
                  </p>
                </answer>
              </task>
              <task>
                <statement>
                  <p>
                    How is the product of these two numbers related to the number of dots in 
                    <m>T_5</m>? Explain why this result is what one might expect from the picture.
                  </p>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                  <p>
                    The product of the number of dots per row (5) and the number of rows (6) is 30. 
                    This equals twice the number of dots in <m>T_5</m> (15), because the rectangle <m>R_5</m> 
                    is formed by two copies of the triangle <m>T_5</m>.
                  </p>
                </answer>
              </task>
              <task>
                <statement>
                  <p>
                    Use your sketches of <m>R_2, R_3, R_4</m> and the provided sketch of <m>R_5</m>
                    to complete <xref ref="table-trinum"/>.
                  </p>
                  <table xml:id="table-trinum">
                    <title>Counting Dots in Triangles and Rectangles</title>
                      <tabular halign="center">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                          <cell>Index <m>n</m></cell>
                          <cell>Number of dots per row</cell>
                          <cell>Number of rows of dots</cell>
                          <cell>Number of dots in <m>R_n</m></cell>
                          <cell>Number of dots in <m>T_n</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                          <cell>1</cell>
                          <cell>1</cell>
                          <cell>2</cell>
                          <cell>2</cell>
                          <cell>1</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                          <cell>2</cell>
                          <cell>2</cell>
                          <cell>3</cell>
                          <cell>6</cell>
                          <cell>3</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                          <cell>3</cell>
                          <cell>?</cell>
                          <cell>?</cell>
                          <cell>?</cell>
                          <cell>?</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                          <cell>4</cell>
                          <cell>?</cell>
                          <cell>?</cell>
                          <cell>?</cell>
                          <cell>?</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                          <cell>5</cell>
                          <cell>?</cell>
                          <cell>?</cell>
                          <cell>?</cell>
                          <cell>?</cell>
                        </row>
                      </tabular>
                  </table>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                  <p>
                    The completed table is shown below.
                  </p>
                  <table xml:id="table-trinum-answer">
                    <title>Counting Dots in Triangles and Rectangles</title>
                    <tabular halign="center">
                      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Index <m>n</m></cell>
                        <cell>Number of dots per row</cell>
                        <cell>Number of rows of dots</cell>
                        <cell>Number of dots in <m>R_n</m></cell>
                        <cell>Number of dots in <m>T_n</m></cell>
                      </row>
                      <row>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                      </row>
                      <row>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>6</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                      </row>
                      <row>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                        <cell>12</cell>
                        <cell>6</cell>
                      </row>
                      <row>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>20</cell>
                        <cell>10</cell>
                      </row>
                      <row>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>5</cell>
                        <cell>6</cell>
                        <cell>30</cell>
                        <cell>15</cell>
                      </row>
                  </tabular>
                </table>
             </answer>
          </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Letting <m>n</m> stand for the number of rows in our triangle <m>T_n</m>,
                give a formula for the total number of dots in <m>T_n</m>. The formula will  
                use the variable <m>n</m>, work for all possible positive integers <m>n</m>, 
                and not require the use of <m>\cdots</m>.
              </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
              <p>
                The total number of dots in <m>T_n</m> is <m>\frac{n(n+1)}{2}</m>.
              </p>
            </answer>
          </task>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <title>Lengths on a GeoBoard</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            A GeoBoard is a rectangular array of dots like the <m>4\times 4</m>-GeoBoard pictured below. 
            We will assume that the horizontal distance between neighboring points is 1 unit and the 
            vertical distance between neighboring points is 1 unit. We will use the Pythagorean Theorem to
            determine diagonal distances. 
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <figure xml:id="image4by4">
          <caption>A <m>4\times 4</m>-GeoBoard.</caption>
        
          <image component="web" source="4by4geoboard.svg" width="50%">
          <shortdescription>
            A grid with 16 dots arranged in four rows and four columns.
          </shortdescription>
          </image>
          <!-- <image component="print" source="4by4geoboard.png" width="50%">
            <shortdescription>
              A grid with 16 dots arranged in four rows and four columns.
            </shortdescription>
          </image>-->
        </figure>
        <p>
          How many different length line segments connect pegs on a GeoBoard? To explore this
          question, we note that the <m>1\times 1</m>-GeoBoard has only one peg and since 
          a line segment needs two endpoints, there are no line segments on a <m>1\times 1</m>-GeoBoard.
        </p>
        <task>
          <introduction>
            <p>
              A <m>2\times 2</m>-GeoBoard consists of four pegs arranged in a square as shown in <xref ref="fig-twobytwogeoboardwithlabels"/>.
              Normally the pegs are unlabeled, but labels are included here for clarity in the discussion that follows.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-twobytwogeoboardwithlabels">
              <caption>A <m>2\times 2</m>-GeoBoard.</caption>
              <image width="30%">
                <shortdescription>Four dots, labeled A, B, C, and D, arranged in a two by two square.</shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="PreFigTwoByTwoGB">
                  <diagram dimensions="(100, 100)" margins="5">
                    <coordinates bbox="(0, 0,4, 4)">
                      <point p="(1,3)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="nw"> <m>A</m> </point>
                      <point p="(3,3)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="ne"> <m>B</m> </point>
                      <point p="(1,1)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="sw"> <m>C</m> </point>
                      <point p="(3,1)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="se"> <m>D</m> </point>
                    </coordinates>
                  </diagram>
                </prefigure>
              </image>
            </figure>
          </introduction>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                By definition, the distance from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m> is 1 unit; in symbols, <m>AB=1</m>. List three other pairs of points in the <m>2\times 2</m>-GeoBoard with
                distance 1 unit.
              </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
              <p>
                <m>AC=1</m>, <m>BD=1</m>, and <m>CD=1</m>.
              </p>
            </answer>
          </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                The distance from <m>A</m> to <m>D</m> is more than 1 unit. Use the Pythagorean Theorem to compute this distance.
                Are there any other pairs of points that are this distance apart?
              </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
              <p>
                The distance from <m>A</m> to <m>D</m> is <m>\sqrt{2}</m>. There is one other pair of points that are this distance apart, namely <m>BC</m>.
              </p>
            </answer>
          </task> 
        </task>
        <task>
          <introduction>
            <p>
              You should be convinced that there were only two distances between pegs on a <m>2\times 2</m>-Geoboard. Now consider the
              <m>3\times 3</m>-Geoboard in <xref ref="fig-threebythreegeoboardwithlabels"/>.
            </p>
              <figure xml:id="fig-threebythreegeoboardwithlabels">
                <caption>A <m>3\times 3</m>-GeoBoard.</caption>
                <image width="30%">
                  <shortdescription>Nine dots, labeled A through I, arranged in a three by three square.</shortdescription>
                  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="PreFigThreeByThreeGB">
                    <diagram dimensions="(200, 200)" margins="5">
                      <coordinates bbox="(0.5, 0.5, 5.5, 5.5)">
                        <point p="(1,5)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="nw"> <m>A</m> </point>
                        <point p="(3,5)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="n"> <m>B</m> </point>
                        <point p="(5,5)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="ne"> <m>C</m> </point>
                        <point p="(1,3)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>D</m> </point>
                        <point p="(3,3)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>E</m> </point>
                        <point p="(5,3)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="e"> <m>F</m> </point>
                        <point p="(1,1)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="sw"> <m>G</m> </point>
                        <point p="(3,1)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="s"> <m>H</m> </point>
                        <point p="(5,1)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="se"> <m>I</m> </point>
                      </coordinates>
                    </diagram>
                  </prefigure>
                </image>
            </figure>
            </introduction>
            <task>
              <statement>
                <p>
                  Again assuming the distance <m>AB</m> is 1 unit, determine <m>AC</m> in the <m>3\times 3</m>-GeoBoard.
                </p>
              </statement>
              <answer>
                <p>
                  <m>AC=2</m>.
                </p>
              </answer>
            </task>
            <task>
              <statement>
                <p>
                  What is the length of <m>\overline{AE}</m>?
                </p>
              </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
              <statement>
                <p>
                  Determine <m>AI</m>.
                </p>
              </statement>
              <answer>
                <p>
                  <m>AI=\sqrt{8}=2\sqrt{2}</m>.
                </p>
              </answer>
            </task>
            <task>
              <statement>
                <p>
                  Use the Pythagorean formula to determine <m>AF</m>. What other point is this distance from <m>A</m>?
                </p>
              </statement>
              <answer>
                <p>
                  <m>AF=\sqrt{5}</m>. The other point that is this distance from <m>A</m> is <m>H</m>.
                </p>
              </answer>
            </task>
            <task>
              <statement>
                <p>
                  Write an argument that would convince a friend that the five segment lengths we found are the only ones possible
                  with a <m>3\times 3</m>-GeoBoard.
                </p>
              </statement>
              <answer>
                <p>
                  Answers will vary.
                </p>
              </answer>
            </task>
            <task>
              <introduction>
                <p>
                  We now turn our attention to a <m>4\times 4</m>-GeoBoard again determining the number of distinct (or different) 
                  segment lengths. You may choose to explore this question on your own or you may use the following sequence of tasks to guide you.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-fourbyfourgeoboardwithlabels">
                  <caption>A <m>4\times 4</m>-GeoBoard.</caption>
                  <image width="30%">
                    <shortdescription>Sixteen dots, labeled A through P, arranged in a four by four square.</shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="PreFigFourByFourGB">
                      <diagram dimensions="(200, 200)" margins="5">
                        <coordinates bbox="(0.5, 0.5, 7.5, 7.5)">
                          <point p="(1,7)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="nw"> <m>A</m> </point>
                          <point p="(3,7)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="nw"> <m>B</m> </point>
                          <point p="(5,7)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="nw"> <m>C</m> </point>
                          <point p="(7,7)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="nw"> <m>D</m> </point>
                          <point p="(1,5)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>E</m> </point>
                          <point p="(3,5)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>F</m> </point>
                          <point p="(5,5)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>G</m> </point>
                          <point p="(7,5)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>H</m> </point>
                          <point p="(1,3)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>I</m> </point>
                          <point p="(3,3)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>J</m> </point>
                          <point p="(5,3)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>K</m> </point>
                          <point p="(7,3)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>L</m> </point>
                          <point p="(1,1)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>M</m> </point>
                          <point p="(3,1)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>N</m> </point>
                          <point p="(5,1)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>O</m> </point>
                          <point p="(7,1)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="w"> <m>P</m> </point>
                        </coordinates>
                      </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                  </image>
                </figure>
              </introduction>
              <task>
                <statement>
                  <p>
                    For the labeled <m>4\times 4</m>-GeoBoard in <xref ref="fig-fourbyfourgeoboardwithlabels"/>, list all of the 
                    whole-number lengths possible. For each of these numbers, identify a segment (or pair of points) with this length
                    (or distance).
                  </p>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                  <p>
                    The whole-number lengths possible are 1, 2, and 3. Examples of segments with these lengths are <m>AB=1</m>, 
                    <m>AC=2</m>, and <m>AD=3</m>.
                  </p>
                </answer>
              </task>
              <task>
                <statement>
                  <p>
                    Some lengths will be multiples of <m>\sqrt{2}</m>. List the whole number multiples of <m>\sqrt{2}</m> possible and give
                    an example of a segment with each length.
                  </p>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                  <p>
                    The whole number multiples of <m>\sqrt{2}</m> possible are <m>2\sqrt{2}</m> and <m>3\sqrt{2}</m>. 
                    Examples of segments with these lengths are <m>AK=2\sqrt{2}</m> and <m>AP=3\sqrt{2}</m>.
                  </p>
                </answer>
              </task>
              <task>
                <statement>
                  <p>
                    Determine <m>AG</m>.
                  </p>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                  <p>
                    <m>AG=\sqrt{5}</m>.
                  </p>
                </answer>
              </task>
              <task>
                <statement>
                  <p>
                    Determine <m>AH</m>.
                  </p>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                  <p>
                    <m>AH=\sqrt{10}</m>.
                  </p>
                </answer>
              </task>
              <task>
                <statement>
                  <p>
                    Determine <m>AL</m>.
                  </p>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                  <p>
                    <m>AL=\sqrt{13}</m>
                  </p>
                </answer>
              </task>
              <task>
                <statement>
                  <p>
                    Are there any other lengths? Explain.
                  </p>
                </statement>
                <answer>
                  <p>
                    No. Explanations will vary.
                  </p>
                </answer>
              </task>
            </task>
        <!--A six by six GeoBoard has a horizontal of length 5 and a diagonal of length 5.This is where the 
        contradiction arises -->
            <task>
              <introduction>
                <p>
                  Let's apply what we already know to the <m>5\times 5</m>-GeoBoard.
                  As illustrated by the green square, there is a <m>4\times 4</m>-GeoBoard inside of 
                  the <m>5\times 5</m>-GeoBoard, so all of the segments and lengths found in
                  the <m>4\times 4</m>-GeoBoard will also be segments and lengths in the <m>5\times 5</m>-Geoboard.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-fivebyfivegeoboardwithlabels">
                  <caption>A <m>5\times 5</m>-GeoBoard containing a <m>4\times 4</m>-GeoBoard.</caption>
                  <image width="30%">
                    <shortdescription>Twenty-five dots arranged in a five by five square. The upper left dot is A.
                      The last column of dots are Q, R, S, T, and U from top to bottom. 
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="PreFigFiveByFiveGB">
                      <diagram dimensions="(200, 200)" margins="5">
                        <coordinates bbox="(0, 0, 10, 10)">
                          <point p="(1,9)" size="1.5" fill="black" alignment="e"> <m>A</m> </point>
                          <point p="(3,9)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(5,9)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(7,9)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(9,9)" size="1.5" fill="black" alignment="e"> <m>Q</m> </point>
                          <point p="(1,7)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(3,7)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(5,7)" size="1.5" fill='black'> </point>
                          <point p="(7,7)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(9,7)" size="1.5" fill="black" alignment="e"> <m>R</m> </point>
                          <point p="(1,5)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(3,5)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(5,5)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(7,5)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(9,5)" size="1.5" fill="black" alignment="e"> <m>S</m> </point>
                          <point p="(1,3)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(3,3)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(5,3)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(7,3)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(9,3)" size="1.5" fill="black" alignment="e"> <m>T</m> </point>
                          <point p="(1,1)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(3,1)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(5,1)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(7,1)" size="1.5" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(9,1)" size="1.5" fill="black" alignment="e"> <m>U</m> </point>
                          <rectangle lower-left="(0.5,2.5)" dimensions="(7,7)" stroke="green" dash="4 4"
                            fill="none"/>
                        </coordinates>
                      </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                  </image>
                </figure>
              </introduction>
                <task>
                  <introduction>
                    <p>
                      Determine the following lengths:
                    </p>
                    </introduction>
                    <task>
                      <statement>
                        <p>
                          <m>AQ</m>
                        </p>
                      </statement>
                      <answer>
                        <p>
                          <m>AQ=4</m>
                        </p>
                      </answer>
                    </task>
                    <task>
                      <statement>
                        <p>
                          <m>AR</m>
                        </p>
                      </statement>
                      <answer>
                        <p>
                          <m>AR=\sqrt{17}</m>
                        </p>
                      </answer>
                    </task>
                    <task>
                      <statement>
                        <p>
                          <m>AS</m>
                        </p>
                      </statement>
                      <answer>
                        <p>
                          <m>AS=\sqrt{20}=2\sqrt{5}</m>
                        </p>
                      </answer> 
                    </task>
                    <task>
                      <statement>
                        <p>
                          <m>AT</m>
                        </p>
                      </statement>
                      <answer>
                        <p>
                          <m>AT=\sqrt{25}=5</m>
                        </p>
                      </answer>
                    </task>
                    <task>
                      <statement>
                        <p>
                          <m>AU</m>
                        </p>
                      </statement>
                      <answer>
                        <p>
                          <m>AU=\sqrt{32}=4\sqrt{2}</m>
                        </p>
                      </answer>
                    </task>
                  </task>
                </task>
                <task>
                  <statement>
                    <p>
                      How many different segment lengths are in a <m>5\times 5</m>-GeoBoard?
                    </p>
                  </statement>
                  <answer>
                    <p>
                      There are 14 different segment lengths in a <m>5\times 5</m>-GeoBoard.
                    </p>
                  </answer>
                </task>
                <task>
                  <statement>
                    <p>
                      Having considered <m>1\times 1</m>-, <m>2\times 2</m>-, <m>3\times 3</m>-,
                      <m>4\times 4</m>-, and <m>5\times 5</m>-GeoBoard, we get <m>0,2,5,9,<fillin fill="14"/></m>
                      for the number of different lengths.
                      Be sure to enter the number for the <m>5\times 5</m>-GeoBoard in the blank provided.
                    </p>
                  </statement>
                </task>
                <task>
                  <statement>
                    <p>
                      Based on our work thus far, how many different segment lengths do you anticipate that a
                      <m>6\times 6</m>-Geoboard should have? Why?
                    </p>
                  </statement>
                  <hint>
                    <p>
                      Look for a pattern!
                    </p>
                  </hint>
                  <answer>
                    <p>
                      It is reasonable to anticipate that a <m>6\times 6</m>-GeoBoard should have 20 different 
                      segment lengths, since the number of different segment lengths seems to be increasing by 
                      2, 3, 4, and 5 as we move from a <m>2\times 2</m>-GeoBoard to a <m>5\times 5</m>-GeoBoard.
                      We will see that this is not the case, however, and that there are only 19 different 
                      segment lengths in a <m>6\times 6</m>-GeoBoard.
                    </p>
                  </answer>
                </task>
            <task>
              <statement>
                <p>
                  You may be surprised to hear that the actual number of different segment lengths in a
                  <m>6\times 6</m>-GeoBoard is actually 19. To see why this happens, compute the distance
                  from point <m>A</m> in the upper left to a new point <m>Y</m> in the sixth column. 
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-sixbysixgeoboardwithlabels">
                  <caption>A <m>6\times 6</m>-GeoBoard containing a <m>5\times 5</m>-GeoBoard.</caption>
                  <image width="40%">
                    <shortdescription>Thirty-six points arranged in a six by six square. The upper left point is labeled A and the 
                      dot in the fourth row and sixth column is labeled Y.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="PreSixBySixGB">
                      <diagram dimensions="(200, 200)" margins="5">
                        <coordinates bbox="(0, 0, 12, 12)">
                          <point p="(1,11)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="e"> <m>A</m> </point>
                          <point p="(3,11)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(5,11)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(7,11)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(9,11)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(11,11)" size="1" fill="black" > </point>
                          <point p="(1,9)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(3,9)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(5,9)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(7,9)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(9,9)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(11,9)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="e"></point>
                          <point p="(1,7)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(3,7)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(5,7)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(7,7)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(9,7)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(11,7)" size="1" fill="black" ></point>
                          <point p="(1,5)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(3,5)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(5,5)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(7,5)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(9,5)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(11,5)" size="1" fill="black" alignment="e"> <m>Y</m> </point>
                          <point p="(1,3)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(3,3)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(5,3)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(7,3)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(9,3)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(11,3)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(1,1)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(3,1)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(5,1)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(7,1)" size="1" fill="black"> </point>
                          <point p="(9,1)" size="1" fill="black"></point>
                          <point p="(11,1)" size="1" fill="black"></point>
                          <rectangle lower-left="(0.5,2.5)" dimensions="(9,9)" stroke="green" dash="4 4"
                            fill="none"/>
                        </coordinates>
                      </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                  </image>
                </figure>
              </statement>
            </task>
          </task>
      </exercise>
    </subexercises>
    <subexercises xml:id="Writing-intro-share">
      <title>
        Writing Prompts
      </title>
      <exercise xml:id="whysqrt8is2sqrt2">
      <title>
        Why is <m>\sqrt{8}</m> equal to <m>2\sqrt{2}</m>?
      </title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In <xref ref="fig-tripfsqrt8"/>, assume <m>DEFB</m> is a square 
          with side length <m>1</m> and that <m>F</m> and <m>D</m> are the midpoints  
          of <m>\overline{AB}</m> and <m>\overline{BC}</m>, respectively.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-tripfsqrt8">
          <caption>Right triangle of side length 2</caption>
          <image source="sqrt-of-8" width="66%">
            <description>
              Isosceles triangle ABC has side length 2. Segment FE joins 
              the midpoints of side AB and hypotenuse AC. Segment DE joins the 
              midpoints of side BC and hypotenuse AC.
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          Write a letter to Aunt Josephine or another friend in which you describe  
          how this picture shows that <m>\sqrt{8}=2\sqrt{2}.</m>
        </p>
        <aside>
          <p>
            Algebraically, we know <m>\sqrt{8}=\sqrt{4\cdot 2}=\sqrt{4}\cdot\sqrt{2}
            =2\sqrt{2}</m>. For this writing prompt, give a geometric proof. There is
            more than one way to do this.  
          </p>
        </aside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="tri-numbers-write">
      <title>Writing about the Count of Dots in a Triangle</title>
        <statement>
        <p>
          Write a letter to a friend in the style of the letter to Aunt Josephine 
          in which you explain why <m>1+2+3+\cdots+n=\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.</m> Your sketches  
          and discoveries in <xref ref="exer-tri-numbers"/> should be helpful in 
          writing this letter.
        </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="writing-intro-share-wonder">
        <title>Wonder in Mathematics</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            After watching the TEDxSidney Talk, <q>Mathematics is the Sense you never
              knew you had
            </q> by high school mathematics teacher Eddie Woo, <xref ref="fig-Woo-TedTalk"/>,
            spend some time looking for, observing, and wondering about patterns 
            and/or similitudes. Possible examples may appear in nature, art, language, 
            numbers, etc. Choose one 
            example and write about what you have observed and wondered. Be sure to 
            discuss how viewing this example and the greater world around us with 
            this sense of maths<fn><sq>Mathematics</sq> is typically called <sq>maths</sq> throughout the United 
              Kingdom and much of the world so it is appropriate for the speaker to use 
            this term.</fn> (as Eddie Woo describes it) can change us and our 
            understanding.
          </p>
          <figure xml:id="fig-Woo-TedTalk">
            <caption>TEDxSydney video on YouTube.</caption>
            <video youtube="PXwStduNw14"/>
          </figure>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subexercises>
  </exercises>
</section>

